lesson k-homes-then-and-now v=1 format=teach grade=0 unit=2 subject=history levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "Homes then and now"
  goal "Say how homes long ago were different from homes now."

  look {
    scene 32x17 {
      thenandnow street year=1850 sign=0 things=["horse and cart"] at=canvas(1, 0)
    }
    say """
        Long ago many homes had no taps, no electric lights and no heaters. Water was carried in from a
        pump or a well, a fire warmed the room and cooked the food, and candles lit the dark. Now water
        comes from taps and light from a switch.
      """
  }
  do {
    level easy {
      worked k.home-then-now k=0 level=medium
    }
    practice k.home-then-now count=3 seed=2 easy-count=2 hard-count=2
    practice k.home-object-age count=3 seed=3 easy-count=2
    show k.street-light-new
    show k.ask-about-home
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show k.why-fireplace
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show k.bath-night
  }
  remember {
    say "Long ago: fires, candles and water from a pump. Now: heaters, lights and taps."
  }

  grown-ups """
    Read each question aloud. This is the first history lesson of the year, before my family: it
    starts from the home the child knows. A visit to an old house or a museum kitchen makes it real,
    and so does asking a grandparent. The facts are general and recalled, not from one source; the
    electric light's date, 1879, is from the light bulb's history the chronicle cites. If the child
    is stuck, ask what would stop working if the electricity went off. England, Reception, ELG Past
    and Present (recalled, not fetched): "Know some similarities and differences between things in
    the past and now, drawing on their experiences and what has been read in class".
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level water from a well is worked in the teacher's pen first, the questions are about water and warmth, and the home things are plainly old or new."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the questions are about washing and lighting, and the home things include a bucket at the well and a scrubbing board."
  }
}
